\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\title{Why study post-training potential?}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{post-training}

\p{Pretraining produces a model that can continue text across many domains.
Subsequent training changes how that model follows instructions, selects
solutions, and acts in an environment. Verifiable tasks make some of these
changes unusually measurable because a checker can judge the outcome of many
rollouts.}

\p{A benchmark improvement does not by itself explain the change. The same
score can result from concentrating probability on an existing strategy,
learning from new feedback, spending more inference compute, or exploiting a
defect in the checker. FTIP asks for experiments and theorems that separate
these cases.}

\p{The intended object is a conditional response surface. Its inputs include
the starting model, the training information, and the permitted computation.
Its outputs are independently measured capabilities and costs. Empirical RLVR
work such as \citef{shao2024deepseekmath}, \citef{guo2025deepseek}, and
\citef{yu2025dapo} supplies motivating cases. These papers do not establish a
universal response surface.}
